- op: discuss  # 2a96
  at: '2026-08-14T10:41:31Z'  # 2a96
  by: agent  # 2a96
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2a96
  clock: 3  # 2a96
  nonce: 2a96  # 2a96
  message: "SATISFIED. Fixed in PR #342, merged to dev as 7812c0b6a0.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, RE-RUN ON THE MERGED TREE (not on the branch, and not a\nproxy metric). Both arms of the defect were live false confirms of a security\nclaim, and both flip:\n\n  BOUNDARY ARM — a C program whose ONLY call is fopen(path,\"w\"), claim\n  {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}:\n      before  rc 0  confirmed   \"No fs_write chains found.\"\n      after   rc 1  violated    \"1 fs_write chain(s) found.\"\n\n  TAINT ARM — getenv(\"API_KEY\") -> fopen(secret,\"w\"), claim\n  {source_taint: host_secret, prohibited_sink_zone: host_fs}:\n      before  rc 0  confirmed\n      after   rc 1  violated\n\nThe isolating fixture matters: a first A/B used a program that also called\nfputs and fclose, which are singly-classified fs_write, so the claim was\nALREADY violated in both arms and only the chain count moved (3 -> 4). That\nwould have been a true measurement of the wrong thing.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IS. The catalogue was always correct -- handed io_mode=\"w\" it\nreturns fs_write. Nothing handed it one: meta[\"io_mode\"] was stamped at exactly\nONE site tree-wide (py.py). C and C++ now stamp it through ONE shared\ntree-sitter helper (analyze.base.stamp_io_mode_from_call) reading a\nlanguage-keyed table in core, beside the catalogue that decides which\nprimitives need a mode. Not a private table per analyzer -- that arrangement IS\nthis invariant.\n\nTHE CONTROLS FOUND THREE THINGS THE ITEM DID NOT.\n\n1. THE STAMP ALONE WOULD HAVE MOVED NOTHING. lookup_with_module has three arms;\n   two called select_by_mode and the third -- the no-module-context arm, which\n   is exactly where C's fopen lands -- handed its candidates to the F3 gate\n   untouched. Narrowed BEFORE the gate now, not after, so the gate still\n   decides what it exists to decide. Checked that narrowing cannot loosen the\n   gate: it keeps one row per mode-discriminated group, so non_method never\n   goes from non-empty to empty, and the ambiguous_names refusal keys on the\n   name, not on hit count.\n\n2. FOUR MORE SINKS WERE BEING DELETED, IN A DIRECTION THE ITEM DID NOT NAME.\n   mode_discriminated_names keyed on the SHORT NAME. rust declares\n   std::fs::File.open (fs_read) and std::fs::OpenOptions.open (fs_write) --\n   two different primitives sharing a name -- so OpenOptions.open inherited a\n   requires_mode no rust analyzer can satisfy, and resolve_mode_boundary(None)\n   is fs_read, so rust's only mode-gated host_fs write sink matched NOTHING in\n   every repo, unconditionally. Same shape for erlang/elixir filelib.ensure_dir,\n   which stats AND creates in one call and has no mode argument at all -- now\n   declared `simultaneous`, the marker that exists for precisely that third\n   shape. The predicate is keyed on (module, name, kind) and the rule lives in\n   one function both the catalogue-wide and per-lookup callers share.\n\n3. C++ IS NOT FIXED, FOR AN UNRELATED AND LARGER REASON -> INV-funuf. Its stamp\n   is correct and presently inert: cpp.py sets the module slot to the\n   comma-joined list of the file's #includes and keeps the header FILENAME\n   while c.yaml declares the STEM, so exact module matching refuses every\n   C-stdlib call. Measured on whisper.cpp: 82,547 call edges, 6 matched, 80\n   recoverable -- and the 80 are getenv, socket/connect/send/recv,\n   fork/execvp/waitpid. A tripwire test goes RED when INV-funuf lands so the\n   disclosure in this repo cannot go stale.\n\nSCOPE CLOSED vs NOT. The item's statement -- \"a primitive whose boundary is\ndecided by io_mode must receive io_mode from the analyzer that emits its call\nedge\" -- now holds for every language whose LIVE catalogue declares a\nmode-discriminated primitive, and a parity test over those catalogues asserts\nit, so the next such row fails there instead of classifying its every write as\na read. The item's \"NOT SWEPT\" note (the other 20 multi-boundary primitives)\nis answered by that census rather than left open: keyed on (module,name,kind)\nand excluding `simultaneous`, the mode-discriminated population is exactly\npython/c/cpp builtins.open + stdio.fopen. rust's `open` was a false positive of\nthe old short-name key and erlang/elixir's ensure_dir was a mis-declaration;\nboth are now correctly outside the set.\n\nGates: suite 22,641 passed / 0 failed, 100% coverage whole-tree AND per-package\nin isolation (core initially FAILED isolation -- the shared helper lives in core\nbut was exercised only from lang-mainstream; fixed with a core-side test that\nstates plainly what a stub does not prove), top-level tests/ 1,542 passed, mypy\nstrict ratchet clean at delta 0."  # 2a96
